Dear 3DPrint.com Editors,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. It is a desktop application for adapting
downloaded 3D models and building printable parts from dimensions, sketches
and tested components. It targets a persistent software gap: a mesh can be
repaired, and a part can be rebuilt in CAD, but changing one practical detail
in a downloaded model is still disproportionately difficult.

Solidon3D 0.3.0 targets that gap. Its most demonstrable new workflow starts
with an imported STL whose mounting hole is wrong. The application recognises
the hole, preserves its location and axis, and changes the diameter as a
reversible operation. A standards-based heat-set insert, screw clearance or
bearing seat can then be added using fit values calibrated from a printed
test piece rather than guessed from a generic table.

Since the first public 0.1 demo, the release history records 220 curated,
user-visible changes. Users can now build reusable parametric parts from
history steps without code, sketch directly in the 3D view, create matching
printable nuts and screws, assign real filament spools with their own process
settings, and pass the correct data through 3MF to the slicer.

Solidon remains local desktop software for Windows, macOS and Linux. It has no
account, cloud project store or telemetry. An optional LLM may call the fixed
operation API, but geometry is always computed by deterministic code and no
generated source is executed.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Detailed changes: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

For editorial evaluation I can provide the original STL, a completed project,
